#
# arch/os/linux — Linux process services.
#
# The code under here acts on a process rather than on data: it decides where
# the system call instructions in a run of code are, writes one byte over each
# of them, and stands behind the trap that byte raises. Nothing in it needs a
# privilege — a process may write to its own text, and the trap it takes is its
# own signal — and nothing in it is out-of-process: there is no ptrace here and
# no second program to attach with.
#
menu "Linux process services"
	depends on PLATFORM = "linux"

config OS_LINUX_IO
	bool "the system call, without a C library to make it through"
	default y
	help
	  _syscall6() and the calls a program makes with it, one _sys_ name
	  each: _sys_read, _sys_write, _sys_open, _sys_close, _sys_readlink,
	  _sys_access, _sys_execve, _sys_exit, _sys_getpid, _sys_getppid,
	  _sys_sendto, _sys_recvfrom, _sys_stat. Beside them, in str.h, are the
	  string helpers a program with no <string.h> still needs: xstr* macros
	  that resolve to the C library's strlen/strcmp/... where a build has a
	  libc and to the hand-written nolibc_ loops here where it does not, plus
	  nolibc_utoa and nolibc_say. That is why this directory is built
	  -fno-builtin: otherwise the compiler turns a nolibc_ loop back into the
	  libc call it was written to replace.

	  This tree links -nostdlib, so tools/ub carries no DT_NEEDED naming a
	  C library. Preloaded that costs nothing: the process being loaded
	  into has one, and every undefined symbol resolves out of it. Run as a
	  program it is the whole difference between starting and not, because
	  ub is entered at its own entry point with no libc under it — so
	  everything ub's program half does, it does through here.

	  The same instruction is what arch/os/linux/sc's dispatcher needs, for
	  the opposite reason: it stands behind an armed system call and must not
	  reach the kernel through the C library, which is what it armed. One
	  definition serves both.

	  hpc's logger is the third caller. Its open and its write go through
	  here whenever this symbol is set (hpc/log/write.c), so a program with
	  no C library can still log, and so an object that interposes on write
	  does not catch its own log lines. Formatting stays libc's.

	  It also carries arch/os/linux/io/nostdlib.c, which is the part of
	  building without a C library that is not a matter of not calling one:
	  six data symbols hpc's own objects refer to (the getopt globals,
	  stdout and stderr) whose relocations the loader resolves when the
	  image is loaded rather than when the code runs. That file says what it
	  does about them and why.

config OS_LINUX_ENTROPY
	bool "the entropy system call, without a C library to make it through"
	default y
	help
	  _sys_getrandom(), the getrandom(2) wrapper, made the same freestanding
	  way arch/os/linux/io makes _sys_read and the rest — one inline system
	  call through io's _syscall3 — so an object linked -nostdlib has a way
	  to draw randomness and a fallback for when the getrandom() symbol it
	  wraps could not be resolved.

	  Beside it is the entropy seam, <arch/os/linux/entropy/hooks.h>: one
	  macro, entropy_hook_fill(), handed the buffer a source just filled,
	  free to read it or to rewrite it in place before the program sees it.
	  It is the same shape as un's TLS hook seam — off by default, and
	  turned into something by the header a build names below — and it is
	  what lets a testing build make getrandom(2) draw a chosen value.

	  Say N and neither the wrapper nor the seam is in the tree; a build
	  that watches io still builds, and simply does not watch entropy.

config OS_LINUX_ENTROPY_HOOKS
	bool
	depends on OS_LINUX_ENTROPY
	help
	  Whether the entropy seam is asked for. Left to a consumer to turn on
	  — modules/ub/entropy does, the way modules/ub/tls turns on un's
	  NET_TLS_HOOKS — because a seam nothing reads is a call to nothing.
	  With it on, the header below names where the definitions come from.

config OS_LINUX_ENTROPY_HOOKS_HEADER
	string
	depends on OS_LINUX_ENTROPY_HOOKS
	help
	  The header <arch/os/linux/entropy/hooks.h> includes, written as it
	  would be inside the angle brackets — a path against one of the
	  build's header roots ("modules/ub/entropy/entropy.h"). Whatever it
	  does not define falls back to the same nothing.

config OS_LINUX_SCAN
	bool "the syscall instruction sites in a run of code"
	depends on ARCH = "x86_64" || ARCH = "arm64"
	default y
	help
	  scan_run(): given a run of machine code and a list of offsets known
	  to be instruction boundaries, say where in it the system call
	  instructions are and, where the preceding instruction settled it,
	  which number each one makes.

	  On x86-64 that needs an instruction length decoder, because
	  `syscall` is two bytes (0f 05) that also occur inside immediates,
	  displacements and data — so the run is swept instruction by
	  instruction from each anchor and a candidate is a site only if the
	  sweep arrives exactly on it. On arm64 instructions are four bytes and
	  aligned, and `svc #0` is one word with nothing else to be mistaken
	  for.

	  It is pure: no system calls, no allocation, no state, and nothing in
	  it cares whether the bytes came from a mapping or from a file. That
	  is what lets the agent and ub-patch share one answer to the same
	  question, one of them at run time and the other at patch time.

config OS_LINUX_SC
	bool "arm the syscall sites in this process and dispatch the trap"
	depends on OS_LINUX_SCAN
	depends on OS_LINUX_IO
	default y
	help
	  sc_open()/sc_arm_self()/sc_close(): walk this process's mappings,
	  turn every syscall instruction the scan above accepted into an
	  undefined instruction, and hand what the resulting SIGILL carries —
	  the number, the six arguments, the site — to one callback.

	  This is how a call is seen at the boundary the kernel draws rather
	  than at the symbol the loader bound: a program that reaches the
	  kernel by inline syscall, or through a libc entry point nothing
	  interposed on, or through sendmsg() and recvmsg(), arrives here all
	  the same. And it needs neither an environment nor a relink, so it
	  works on a process that is already running.

	  What it costs is a signal per watched call, which is why only the
	  sites the scan could not rule out are armed: a site whose preceding
	  instruction says it makes clock_gettime() is left alone, and one
	  whose number is not decidable is armed and reverts itself the first
	  time it turns out to make a call that must not be made from a signal
	  handler.

	  arch/os/linux/README.md has the whole of it, including the two things it
	  will not survive: a program that installs its own SIGILL handler
	  after this one, and a debugger that stops on the trap.

endmenu
